# NJ Flood Exposure Explorer: working rules

## What this is
A small, focused web app answering one question: "How flood-exposed is my NJ town,
and who bears it?" Interactive map (FEMA flood zones over NJDEP overburdened
communities), click a municipality to get a plain-language summary with one chart
and one table. Nothing else.

## Scope discipline (the most important rule)
- The scope is defined by docs/PLAN.md (S1–S7 and P1–P4 are complete). Do not
  expand past that plan unless a new slice is written there first.
- Any feature idea outside the plan goes into FUTURE.md, never into code.
- Do not add libraries, abstractions, config options, or "while we're here"
  improvements that the current slice does not need.

## Data rules
- The ONLY data sources are the three services in src/config/sources.ts. Their URLs
  and field names were manually verified in a browser. NEVER invent, guess, or
  substitute service URLs, layer IDs, or field names; if something seems wrong,
  stop and say so instead of improvising.
- Every user-facing number must be traceable to a source; the UI links sources.
- Legal definitions (e.g. "overburdened community", N.J.S.A. 13:1D-158) are quoted
  from the statute / NJDEP, never paraphrased into new thresholds.

## Code conventions
- React function components, TypeScript strict, no class components.
- ArcGIS Maps SDK (@arcgis/core) for all mapping; Recharts for the chart.
- Small files, plain names. No premature abstraction: duplicate twice before
  extracting a helper.
- Comments only for constraints the code cannot express (projection quirks,
  service record limits). No narrative comments.

## Process
- One slice per session; each slice ends with the acceptance criterion from
  docs/PLAN.md verified in the running app, then a commit.
- Commit messages: short imperative summary of what changed, nothing else.
- Record every non-obvious choice in DECISIONS.md at the moment it is made
  (what was chosen, what was rejected, why).
